#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/id #uuid "baae925b-d23d-56db-b15f-b438f68df608" :question/math-academy-id "e-4101" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $3^{3x-3}=9^x$." :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${\\color{red}3}^2$, and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{3x - 3} &= 9^{x} \\\\\n3^{3x - 3} &= (3^{2})^{x} \\\\\n3^{3x - 3} &= 3^{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3})$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 3 &= 2x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114965 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "e73675af-973f-583e-9909-0eb9630fdcc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4161" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $9^{3x}=27^{x+2}$." :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using\n\n$$\n9={\\color{red}3}^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n27 = {\\color{red}3}^3\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{red}3}^2\\right)^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^3\\right)^{x+2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{2(3x)} = 3^{3(x + 2)}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3})$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(3x) &= 3(x + 2) \\\\\n6x &= 3x + 6 \\\\\n3x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "618a3d18-0334-5e5f-bf2e-a11a27c6998c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244062} {:answer/id #uuid "4c595474-8fe6-5c9f-9e44-93e1d33b3a88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244063} {:answer/id #uuid "a877d75f-1f12-58f4-83b1-1138231651f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244064} {:answer/id #uuid "f51a2a9d-aa08-59d2-8de3-146721680d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244065} {:answer/id #uuid "44e9f8c5-1f0d-51b1-a537-e42625d748c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a877d75f-1f12-58f4-83b1-1138231651f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244064} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1fe442-8b64-5d08-a6c0-bdfcd15919c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78892017-6fcd-5bd8-a72d-1a9de112b877" :question/math-academy-id "q-100449" :question/problem "If ${8}^{5a-12}=\\frac{1}{{64}^{a+4}}$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $64$ as ${8}^{2},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{64}^{a+4}} \\\\ {8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{({8}^{2})}^{a+4}} \\\\ {8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{8}^{2(a+4)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{5a-12} & ={8}^{-2(a+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(8)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-12 & =-2(a+4) \\\\ 5a-12 & =-2a-8 \\\\ 7a & =4 \\\\ a & =\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8edd1a6-5738-59a9-8098-e32b8df50fa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244069} {:answer/id #uuid "8e266992-9d47-5927-8455-7c9c0df8558c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244070} {:answer/id #uuid "5d95b726-ef43-51ef-b008-3b7719c80167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244071} {:answer/id #uuid "cf481cb9-f177-5f7f-904e-ea442d43fe25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244072} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9955be-10c4-5296-8f86-34f2eaccebae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9955be-10c4-5296-8f86-34f2eaccebae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244073} :answer-field/id #uuid "b001aad1-4216-5585-af6a-b153890f2e70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83470321-b83a-54ab-9fd1-5a4497a9619c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100468" :question/problem "Find the value of $d$ that satisfies the equation ${25}^{d-7}={625}^{d+10}.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $25={5}^{2}$ and $625={5}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{2})}^{d-7}={({5}^{4})}^{d+10}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2(d-7)}={5}^{4(d+10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(d-7) & =4(d+10) \\\\ 2d-14 & =4d+40 \\\\ -2d & =54 \\\\ d & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c79d5ea-620d-5f46-8e2a-0cc77b8a5a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244076} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c807df-e71e-5a4f-af92-24d496440270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244077} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b5ffec-635f-54a1-92e0-765a82a62284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244078} {:answer/id #uuid "b001678a-1c1a-5dce-a7b0-9d3ab17a6ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244079} {:answer/id #uuid "0b48d8dc-25ee-5188-abc9-a2f92b13f2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b48d8dc-25ee-5188-abc9-a2f92b13f2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244080} :answer-field/id #uuid "41fa3146-0439-526f-8a2b-79cf92f3b238" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "028d8773-8ea7-5c3f-b517-f8c76313f6d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-110537" :question/problem "If ${4}^{k-1}={64}^{2k}$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $64$ as ${4}^{3},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{k-1} & ={64}^{2k} \\\\ {4}^{k-1} & ={({4}^{3})}^{2k} \\\\ {4}^{k-1} & ={4}^{6k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(4),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-1 & =6k \\\\ -1 & =5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0a3c79b-a8a3-5eeb-bea5-08027152a777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244083} {:answer/id #uuid "43d22559-7222-5d9b-9b0c-4af03009b012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244084} {:answer/id #uuid "57fbc75f-e073-51bf-942c-e908d9fe18fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244085} {:answer/id #uuid "5688c327-8b63-5aa3-8119-8ae6928df5d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244086} {:answer/id #uuid "d901fbe5-6bf5-5f32-981b-4e87e9f832f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 1